            <ArticleTitle>Rare case of a paratubal cystadenoma with bilateral hydrosalpinges in an infertile woman.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>239-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.tjog.2014.04.020</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1028-4559(14)00080-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Serous tumors can occur on the surface of the ovaries and-rarely-as primary tumors on the peritoneal surface. However, the origin of extraovarian serous tumors is unknown. An unusual case of a serous tumor is presented here.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 34-year-old nulligravid woman presented for evaluation of her primary infertility, with acute abdominal pain. At laparoscopy, a paratubal cyst, independent of the ovaries, was found to be associated with bilateral hydrosalpinges. The final pathology diagnosis was of a benign paratubal serous cystadenoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We present an unusual case of a paratubal serous cystadenoma with bilateral hydrosalpinges in an infertile woman, which was resected laparoscopically..</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2014. Published by Elsevier B.V.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
